[[:db/cas 17592186542434 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $8x$ term that can cancel with the $-8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 2y &= 16 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= -16 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + y &= 8 \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{8 - 4x}{1} \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,8 - 4u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $8x$ term that can cancel with the $-8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 2y &= 16 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= -16 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + y &= 8 \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{8 - 4x}{1} \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,8 - 4x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542454 :answer/value "(u, -4u + 8)" "(x, -4x + 8)"] [:db/cas 17592186542467 :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u + 2)" "(x, -\\frac{1}{4}x + 2)"] [:db/cas 17592186542474 :answer/value "(u, 8)" "(x, 8)"] [:db/cas 17592186542483 :answer/value "(u, 4u + 8)" "(x, 4x + 8)"] [:db/cas 17592186542497 :answer/value "(u, -4u - 8)" "(x, -4x - 8)"] [:db/ensure 17592186542434 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186542486 :answer-field/validate] [:db/cas 17592186542435 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 && \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 16y &= 12 \\\\\n-4x + 16y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (12 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n-4y &= 3 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 - x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 && \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 16y &= 12 \\\\\n-4x + 16y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (12 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n-4y &= 3 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 - x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542475 :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u + \\frac{3}{4})" "(x, \\frac{1}{4}x + \\frac{3}{4})"] [:db/cas 17592186542494 :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u - \\frac{3}{4})" "(x, \\frac{1}{4}x - \\frac{3}{4})"] [:db/cas 17592186542495 :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u - \\frac{3}{4})" "(x, -\\frac{1}{4}x - \\frac{3}{4})"] [:db/cas 17592186542503 :answer/value "(u, 4u + 3)" "(x, 4x + 3)"] [:db/cas 17592186542511 :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{3}{4})" "(x, -\\frac{3}{4})"] [:db/ensure 17592186542435 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186542436 :answer-field/validate] [:db/cas 17592186542442 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 && \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 6y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= 30 \\\\\n0 &= (-30 + 30) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n-2y &= 10 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10 - x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= -5 + \\frac{1}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,-5 + \\frac{1}{2}t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 && \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 6y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= 30 \\\\\n0 &= (-30 + 30) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n-2y &= 10 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10 - x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= -5 + \\frac{1}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-5 + \\frac{1}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542444 :answer/value "(t, 2t + 10)" "(x, 2x + 10)"] [:db/cas 17592186542446 :answer/value "(t, \\frac{1}{2}t - 5)" "(x, \\frac{1}{2}x - 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186542453 :answer/value "(t, -5)" "(x, -5)"] [:db/cas 17592186542473 :answer/value "(t, -\\frac{1}{2}t - 5)" "(x, -\\frac{1}{2}x - 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186542504 :answer/value "(t, \\frac{1}{2}t + 5)" "(x, \\frac{1}{2}x + 5)"] [:db/ensure 17592186542442 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186542440 :answer-field/validate] [:db/cas 17592186542448 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -2 \\\\\n0 &= (2 - 2) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 1 \\\\\n-y &= 1 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= -1 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-1 + 2u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -2 \\\\\n0 &= (2 - 2) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 1 \\\\\n-y &= 1 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= -1 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-1 + 2x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542451 :answer/value "(u, -1)" "(x, -1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542472 :answer/value "(u, -2u - 1)" "(x, -2x - 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542481 :answer/value "(u, 2u + 1)" "(x, 2x + 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542490 :answer/value "(u, 2u - 1)" "(x, 2x - 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542508 :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{2}u + \\frac{1}{2})" "(x, \\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2})"] [:db/ensure 17592186542448 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186542499 :answer-field/validate] [:db/cas 17592186542452 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\[6pt]\n6x - 3y &= -18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpress your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are\n\n$(t,$ {{field-1}} $),$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number." "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the following lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\[6pt]\n6x - 3y &= -18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpress your answer in terms of $x$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are\n\n$(x,$ {{field-1}} $),$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542452 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 && \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 3y &= 18 \\\\\n6x - 3y &= -18 \\\\\n0 &= (18 - 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\\n-y &= -6 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= 6 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,6 + 2t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 && \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 3y &= 18 \\\\\n6x - 3y &= -18 \\\\\n0 &= (18 - 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\\n-y &= -6 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= 6 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,6 + 2x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542458 :answer/value "2t + 6" "2x + 6"] [:db/ensure 17592186542452 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186542498 :answer-field/validate] [:db/cas 17592186542470 :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 8y &= -8 \\\\\n2x + 8y &= 8 \\\\\n0 &= (-8 + 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n-4y &= -4 + x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-4 + x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,1 - \\frac{1}{4}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number." "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 && \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 8y &= -8 \\\\\n2x + 8y &= 8 \\\\\n0 &= (-8 + 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n-4y &= -4 + x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-4 + x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1 - \\frac{1}{4}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."] [:db/cas 17592186542464 :answer/value "(u, -4u + 4)" "(x, -4x + 4)"] [:db/cas 17592186542469 :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u - 1)" "(x, -\\frac{1}{4}x - 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542487 :answer/value "(u, \\frac{1}{4}u + 1)" "(x, \\frac{1}{4}x + 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542493 :answer/value "(u, -\\frac{1}{4}u + 1)" "(x, -\\frac{1}{4}x + 1)"] [:db/cas 17592186542501 :answer/value "(u, 1)" "(x,